<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net472;net8.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <RootNamespace>PSADT.ClientServer.Server.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\PSADT.ClientServer.Server\PSADT.ClientServer.Server.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ClientServerUtilities' static constructor calls IsAuthenticodeTrusted() on each client/server
    executable, which throws when the file is missing. Anything that reads it - which for this project
    means ServerInstance itself - then fails with a TypeInitializationException instead. The four
    executables therefore have to sit beside the test assembly, exactly as they do for PSADT.Tests.

    The lookup resolves to the net472 directory beside PSADT.dll, so both of this project's target
    frameworks land on the same folder: net472 is its own output directory, and net8.0 walks up one
    level and back down into net472. Building the four projects and copying their executables there
    once therefore satisfies both runs.

    Ordering is expressed as a ProjectReference rather than by invoking the Build target imperatively.
    An MSBuild task asking for Build races the solution's own build of the same project and the two
    collide writing its output; a ProjectReference goes through MSBuild's project instance cache and
    builds once. GetTargetPath below only reads a property, so it adds no second builder.

    UndefineProperties drops TargetFramework from what the reference passes down, and it has to. These
    four projects are single-targeted, so a solution build builds them with no TargetFramework global
    property at all. Handing them one - which is what SetTargetFramework does - produces a second,
    differently-keyed project instance that the instance cache cannot match against the first, and the
    two then build concurrently into the same output. Undefining it makes both requests the same
    instance.

    Tests that need these executables still gate on TestEnvironment.ClientServerExecutablesPresent, so
    a build that skips this - "dotnet build -f net8.0" addresses the other inner build and never runs
    it - reports the gap rather than failing on type initialization.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ClientServerProject Include="..\..\src\PSADT.ClientServer.Client\PSADT.ClientServer.Client.csproj" />
    <ClientServerProject Include="..\..\src\PSADT.ClientServer.Client.Compatible\PSADT.ClientServer.Client.Compatible.csproj" />
    <ClientServerProject Include="..\..\src\PSADT.ClientServer.Client.Launcher\PSADT.ClientServer.Client.Launcher.csproj" />
    <ClientServerProject Include="..\..\src\PSADT.ClientServer.Client.Launcher.Compatible\PSADT.ClientServer.Client.Launcher.Compatible.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net472'">
    <ProjectReference Include="@(ClientServerProject)" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" UndefineProperties="TargetFramework" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The executables alone are not enough. A client that is only launched and then shut down still loads the
    assembly holding the dialog implementations on its way out, so a run that copied the four executables by
    themselves got as far as talking to a client and then failed disposing of it, on an assembly that was
    never laid down beside them. The shipped module puts the whole set in one directory; this reproduces
    that by asking each project for its built output and for everything it copies alongside it, rather than
    for its target path alone.
  -->
  <Target Name="CopyClientServerExecutables" AfterTargets="Build" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net472'">
    <MSBuild Projects="@(ClientServerProject)" Targets="BuiltProjectOutputGroup;ReferenceCopyLocalPathsOutputGroup" Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration)" RemoveProperties="TargetFramework" BuildInParallel="false">
      <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="_ClientServerOutput" />
    </MSBuild>
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(_ClientServerOutput)" DestinationFolder="$(OutputPath)" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

</Project>
